{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791189578,"nonce":"GteAwGvOwxUOkSuoA8+/JJwfBs8=","title":"Engine simplification: adopt the model ashui's blinc_abi proves, not its line count","message":"Prompted by how much ashui (a Haxe UI on a 6k-line Rust ABI over a slice of\nBlinc) does with so little. What follows is measured from both trees, with\nthe limits stated. It is an evaluation, not a plan: nothing here is decided.\n\n\nblinc_abi is about 6,100 lines of Rust. It depends on blinc_core,\nblinc_layout, blinc_text, blinc_svg and blinc_image, and on none of\nblinc_app, blinc_gpu, blinc_css or blinc_cn. From blinc_layout it uses\nLayoutTree, RenderProps, the PropertyId binding registry, the two\nqueue_*_update_partial functions and the text measurer: roughly 5% of\nthat crate. The rest of ashui is Haxe: about 51k lines, of which a CSS\nengine is 6k, Tailwind classes 1.1k, input state 0.8k, and the renderer\nand shaders 5k.\n\nIts size is partly borrowed. It reuses Blinc's layout, text, svg and image\ncode and renders through hlwgpu, so 6k is not a like-for-like figure.\nWhat transfers is the model.\n\n\n1. One property channel. Every property is a PropertyId with a typed value\n   that is a constant, a signal or a computed. A single table maps\n   (PropertyId, value type) to a write into Taffy's Style or into\n   RenderProps. That table is layout_router.rs, about 950 lines, and it is\n   the only place a property is plumbed.\n\n2. CSS and element code write through the same channel. A node has two\n   writers: set, which claims the property for the element, and style,\n   which writes it for a stylesheet unless the element owns it. That is\n   Tailwind's layering, and it replaces a parallel style model. Each CSS\n   handler returns the fields it wrote so the cascade can unset the ones a\n   later match stops writing. Transitions interpose on the same apply.\n\n3. Tailwind classes are compile-time sugar over that channel. tw(\"p-4\n   hover:bg-primary\") expands to plain node.set calls. A state variant\n   expands to node.set(Prop, Computed.make(() -\u003e hover ? a : b)). Hover is\n   a computed over a per-node boolean signal, so no subtree is rebuilt.\n\n4. No state machines. A node's whole interaction state is eight boolean\n   signals (hovered, pressed, focused, focusVisible, focusWithin,\n   disabled, checked, indeterminate). The input layer is 769 lines.\n\n5. Nodes persist and components run once. Structural change is two\n   components, For and Show, 85 lines together, each item under its own\n   Owner for cleanup. An item whose value survives keeps its element.\n   This is Solid's model, not React's.\n\n\nProperty plumbing. letter_spacing touches 23 source files in Blinc\n(excluding tests, examples, blinc_gpu and blinc_text) and 10 in ashui,\nand several of ashui's are consumers (glyph layout, canvas outlines,\ntransitions) rather than plumbing. The visual structs are triplicated:\nRenderProps, DynRenderProps and ResolvedRenderProps each carry shadow,\nopacity and the rest, and element.rs mentions shadow eight times.\n\nA finished replacement already exists and is half-adopted. binding.rs\n(register_typed and friends, about 2,000 lines) is the same channel, and\nits own doc comment says Div builder methods \"will call internally in\nP2.2+\". They mostly do not. DynRenderProps and ResolvedRenderProps are\nconstructed nowhere in crates, packages or examples (a 234-line section\nof element.rs; value.rs is separately used by the animation scheduler, so\nthat file is not dead).\n\nState styling. Blinc has three mechanisms at once: stateful closures\n(on_state 106 sites, stateful_with_key 60, use_state_keyed 110, a 5,730-line\nstateful.rs), CSS :hover through the stylesheet pass, and bound props.\nblinc_cn splits 162 class-styled sites against 80 stateful ones, and nearly\nevery component uses both. dropdown_menu.rs already documents moving away\nfrom stateful per widget, in a comment.\n\nIdentity reconstruction. This is the largest gap and the one behind the\ndropdown symptom. Blinc rebuilds, then reconstructs identity afterwards.\nLayoutNodeId is a slotmap key that every full rebuild regenerates.\nStableNodeId is re-derived each frame as a hash of (parent id, explicit key\nor sibling index), so an unkeyed node's identity is its position and an\nancestor shift moves the whole subtree. Around that sit mint_stable_ids_walk,\nthree sweeps (handlers, CSS animations, transitions),\nauto_fill_animation_stable_keys, build_generation, InstanceKey so keyed\nstate can be found again, and diff.rs plus its siblings, 2,890 lines.\nRoughly 500 references across the tree (some lines match more than one\npattern). ashui has none, because nothing is rebuilt.\n\n\nEstablished from the code, not reproduced in a running dropdown:\n\n- start_all_css_animations scans registered nodes after a build and starts\n  the animation on any node with an animation: rule whose StableNodeId has\n  no entry in css_anim_store. A settled animation does stay in the store\n  as a same-target guard.\n- So a replay needs the node's stable id to change or its entry to be\n  swept. Both are reachable: unkeyed ids move with sibling index, and\n  sweep_stale_css_animations drops entries whose id has no mapping after a\n  rebuild.\n- dropdown_menu.rs says, in a comment, that stateful rebuilds inside an\n  overlay content closure re-enter the motion FSM and freeze base styles on\n  hover, and it avoids Stateful for rows for that reason. The sweep's own\n  comment cites an earlier cn-context-menu submenu-hover opacity flicker\n  that an eager drain caused. This class of bug has been patched before.\n\nNot established: that the specific text replay is this path, rather than a\nsibling of it. A reproducer (animated node, insert a sibling before it,\nrebuild, check whether the animation restarts) would settle it.\n\n\n- ashui keeps one tree for the whole process, behind an unsafe cell. Blinc\n  is multi-window, and multi-window is where it has been bitten before.\n- For matches items by == with a linear scan, so it is quadratic and\n  reference-identity for objects.\n- Lifecycle there leans on the host's GC finalizers; Blinc would use Owner\n  scopes and RAII.\n- CSS coverage parity is NOT established. ashui's CSS engine is 6,059 lines\n  against about 27,800 in Blinc (blinc_css 20,755 plus 7,051 of stylesheet\n  application and selectors), but ashui registers properties through loops\n  and data tables, so a count of its handlers by grep undercounts, and I\n  cannot claim equal coverage at a quarter of the code.\n- Most of Blinc's 408k lines is product scope ashui does not have, not\n  architecture: GPU renderer 37k, app shell 32k, DSL 36k, icons 27k,\n  widgets 35k, packages 53k, cn 33k. The engine layer to compare is\n  blinc_core plus blinc_layout minus widgets plus blinc_css, about 104k.\n\n\nEach step can land alone and each shrinks the next.\n\n1. Finish moving Div builders onto the binding channel, then delete\n   DynRenderProps and ResolvedRenderProps. Lowest risk, and the doc\n   comment already promises it.\n2. Route CSS through the channel with element-owned versus stylesheet-owned\n   writes, retiring ElementStyle-to-RenderProps merging. This is the\n   23-files-to-about-4 step and the one that removes the stateful merge\n   landmine.\n3. State variants as computeds over per-node interaction signals. cn\n   components migrate one at a time; FSM stays for logic.\n4. Persistent nodes with For, Show and Owner. Highest value and highest\n   risk: it changes the programming model of every widget, cn and the DSL.\n   Do it for new widgets first and let the two models coexist. Until the\n   existing widgets move, the 95% of blinc_layout ashui bypasses cannot be\n   deleted, because Blinc's own widgets sit on it.\n\nRelated: 2103fd6 (reactive architecture v2), 12cb642.","files":null}]}